{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KadRqzXwZMRmFuprYpot1T","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n4\t\n5\tIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n6\t\n7\tPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n8\t1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n9\t2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n10\t3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n11\t4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n12\t5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n13\t\n14\tQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n15\t- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n16\t- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n17\t- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n18\t- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n19\t\n20\tNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n21\t\n22\tMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n23\t\n24\tInline Math ($...$ format):\n25\t- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n26\t- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n27\t- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n28\t- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n29\t\n30\tDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n31\t- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n32\t- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n33\t- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n34\t- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n35\t- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n36\t- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n37\t\n38\tFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n39\t- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n40\t- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n41\t- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n42\t- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n43\t\n44\tMath Notation Standards:\n45\t- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n46\t- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n47\t- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n48\t- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n49\t- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n50\t\n51\tPROSE STYLE:\n52\t\n53\tProfessional/Academic Voice:\n54\t- Impersonal, objective tone\n55\t- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n56\t- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n57\t- Remove instructional tone\n58\t- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n59\t\n60\tProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n61\t- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n62\t- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n63\t- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n64\t- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n65\t\n66\tProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n67\t- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n68\t- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n69\t- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n70\t\n71\tCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n72\t- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n73\t- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n74\t- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n75\t- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n76\t- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n77\t\n78\tDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n79\t- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n80\t- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n81\t- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n82\t\n83\tCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n84\t\n85\tMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n86\t- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n87\t- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n88\t- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n89\t- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n90\t- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n91\t\n92\tDeduplication:\n93\t- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n94\t- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n95\t- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n96\t\n97\tCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n98\t- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n99\t- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n100\t- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n101\t\n102\tFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n103\t- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n104\t- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n105\t\n106\tOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\n107\tProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n108\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"56163956-99ba-4c7e-a2a8-4e65a250d9cf","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:58:58.352Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/format.session.md","content":"PROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n\nPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n\nIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n\nPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n\nQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n\nNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n\nMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n\nInline Math ($...$ format):\n- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n\nDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n\nFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n\nMath Notation Standards:\n- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n\nPROSE STYLE:\n\nProfessional/Academic Voice:\n- Impersonal, objective tone\n- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n- Remove instructional tone\n- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n\nProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n\nProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n\nCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n\nDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n\nCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n\nMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n\nDeduplication:\n- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n\nCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n\nFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n\nOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\nProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
