{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R9X37qns7qLZP8UgWeNHfE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## MCER and ELastic Design Spectra (ASCE) {#sec-mcer}\n2\t\n3\tSeismic demand for all Risk Categories I–IV was derived in accordance with **ASCE/SEI 7-22**—as adopted by **IBC 2021**—using the site-specific uniform-hazard spectrum (UHS) established in the preceding chapter. Chapter 11 of ASCE 7 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations by values obtained from a probabilistic hazard analysis coupled with site-response modelling, provided the procedures of §11.4.7 are satisfied [@ASCE722 §11.4.7]. \n4\t\n5\tChapter §11.4.7 indicate that to perform a site response analysis and a ground motion hazard analysis to determine ground motions for any structure procedures of sections §21.1 and §21.2 must be applied. \n6\t\n7\tWhen the procedures of either Section §21.1 or §21.2 are used, the design response spectrum shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.3, the design acceleration parameters shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.4.\n8\t\n9\tThe probabilistic spectral response accelerations shall be taken as the spectral response accelerations in the direction of maximum horizontal response represented by a 5% damped acceleration response spectrum that is expected to achieve a 1% probability of collapse within a 50-year period. If the spectral response accelerations predicted by the ground motion models do not represent the maximum response in the horizontal plane, then the response spectral accelerations computed from the hazard analysis shall be scaled by factors to increase the motions to the maximum response. If the ground motion models predict the geometric mean or similar metric of the two horizontal components, then the scale factors shall be 1.2 for periods less than, or equal to, 0.2 s, 1.25 for a period of 1.0 s, and 1.3 for periods greater than or equal to 10 s.\n10\t\n11\tConsistent with §21.3, the **design-level spectral accelerations** are obtained by scaling the MCER ordinates by the factor $2/3$ [@ASCE722 Eq. 21.3-1]:\n12\t\n13\t$$\n14\tS_{a}=\\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}\n15\t$$\n16\t\n17\tWhen a site-specific procedure is used to determine the design ground motion the design acceleration parameters are calculated as [@ASCE722 §21.4]: \n18\t$$\n19\t  S_{DS} = 0.9 \\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[0.2,\\,5\\,\\text{s}]} S_a(T)\n20\t  $$ $$\n21\t  S_{D1} = \\max\\!\\left(0.9\\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[1,\\,T^*]\\,\\text{s}} \\left[T \\cdot S_a(T)\\right],\\quad\n22\t  S_a(1\\,\\text{s})\\right)\n23\t  \\quad \\text{con} \\quad\n24\t  T^* = \\begin{cases} 2\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s > 442\\,\\text{m/s} \\\\ 5\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s \\leq\n25\t  442\\,\\text{m/s} \\end{cases}\n26\t  $$\n27\tThe spectral response acceleration parameter at short periods ($S_{MS}$) and the spectral response acceleration parameter at a period of 1 s ($S_{M1}$) are calculated as:\n28\t\n29\t$$\n30\tS_{MS}= 1.5 S_{DS}, \\qquad\n31\tS_{M1}=1.5 S_{M1}\n32\t$$\n33\t\n34\t\n35\t\n36\t\n37\t\n38\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"8b61e266-0afe-456b-a531-8368a881700f","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:59:06.272Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/psha_hive.kb.prepared/criteria_MCER.qmd","content":"## MCER and ELastic Design Spectra (ASCE) {#sec-mcer}\n\nSeismic demand for all Risk Categories I–IV was derived in accordance with **ASCE/SEI 7-22**—as adopted by **IBC 2021**—using the site-specific uniform-hazard spectrum (UHS) established in the preceding chapter. Chapter 11 of ASCE 7 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations by values obtained from a probabilistic hazard analysis coupled with site-response modelling, provided the procedures of §11.4.7 are satisfied [@ASCE722 §11.4.7]. \n\nChapter §11.4.7 indicate that to perform a site response analysis and a ground motion hazard analysis to determine ground motions for any structure procedures of sections §21.1 and §21.2 must be applied. \n\nWhen the procedures of either Section §21.1 or §21.2 are used, the design response spectrum shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.3, the design acceleration parameters shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.4.\n\nThe probabilistic spectral response accelerations shall be taken as the spectral response accelerations in the direction of maximum horizontal response represented by a 5% damped acceleration response spectrum that is expected to achieve a 1% probability of collapse within a 50-year period. If the spectral response accelerations predicted by the ground motion models do not represent the maximum response in the horizontal plane, then the response spectral accelerations computed from the hazard analysis shall be scaled by factors to increase the motions to the maximum response. If the ground motion models predict the geometric mean or similar metric of the two horizontal components, then the scale factors shall be 1.2 for periods less than, or equal to, 0.2 s, 1.25 for a period of 1.0 s, and 1.3 for periods greater than or equal to 10 s.\n\nConsistent with §21.3, the **design-level spectral accelerations** are obtained by scaling the MCER ordinates by the factor $2/3$ [@ASCE722 Eq. 21.3-1]:\n\n$$\nS_{a}=\\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}\n$$\n\nWhen a site-specific procedure is used to determine the design ground motion the design acceleration parameters are calculated as [@ASCE722 §21.4]: \n$$\n  S_{DS} = 0.9 \\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[0.2,\\,5\\,\\text{s}]} S_a(T)\n  $$ $$\n  S_{D1} = \\max\\!\\left(0.9\\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[1,\\,T^*]\\,\\text{s}} \\left[T \\cdot S_a(T)\\right],\\quad\n  S_a(1\\,\\text{s})\\right)\n  \\quad \\text{con} \\quad\n  T^* = \\begin{cases} 2\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s > 442\\,\\text{m/s} \\\\ 5\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s \\leq\n  442\\,\\text{m/s} \\end{cases}\n  $$\nThe spectral response acceleration parameter at short periods ($S_{MS}$) and the spectral response acceleration parameter at a period of 1 s ($S_{M1}$) are calculated as:\n\n$$\nS_{MS}= 1.5 S_{DS}, \\qquad\nS_{M1}=1.5 S_{M1}\n$$\n\n\n\n\n\n","numLines":38,"startLine":1,"totalLines":38}}}
