{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018CRbEGoTuse7VGXkGjyvi8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7-22 section 21.3 design response spectrum MCER two-thirds factor Sa SaM\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Is Continued Use of the Two-Period Design Spectrum in the Equivalent Lateral Force Procedure of Seismic Design Permitted by ASCE 7-22? - ICC\",\"url\":\"https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Multi-Period Response Spectrum\",\"url\":\"https://www.oregon.gov/bcd/codes-stand/Documents/sam-24-03-multi-period-response.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"S. K. Ghosh Associates LLC - Seismic & Building Code Consulting\",\"url\":\"https://www.skghoshassociates.com/blog/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1\"},{\"title\":\"Ground Motion Levels per ASCE 7-16 with ideCAD\",\"url\":\"https://help.idecad.com/ideCAD/earthquake-ground-motion-levels-1\"},{\"title\":\"Multi-Period Response Spectra and Design Parameters (of ...\",\"url\":\"https://files.scec.org/s3fs-public/2018_UGMS_1300_Kircher_BSSC_multi-period_spectra_and_design_parameters.pdf?.ArgamOa6ZWLtsDekb9Dn6w1Lg6VBYDC=\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Spectra Development\",\"url\":\"https://quakemanager.eqsols.com/topics/idh-asce-7-22.htm\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788\"},{\"title\":\"Response spectrum curves (ASCE)\",\"url\":\"https://www.graitec.com/Help/Advance_Design/En/response_spectrum_curves_(asce).htm\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about ASCE 7-22 Section 21.3 and the two-thirds factor relationship with MCER response spectra:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nWhere a site-specific ground motion analysis is performed in accordance with Section 11.4.7, the design response spectrum is to be as indicated in Section 21.3.\n\nRegarding the two-thirds factor and the relationship between SDS/Sa and MCER parameters:\n\nIn ASCE 7-22, SDS is (2/3)SMS and SMS is taken as 90% of the maximum spectral response acceleration, Sa, at any period within the range from 0.2 to 5 s, in accordance with ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4.\n\nSimilarly, SD1 is (2/3) SM1 and SM1 is defined as 90% of the maximum value of the product TSa over periods from 1 to 2 s for sites with shear wave velocity, vs>1,450 ft/s (stiff to moderately stiff soils), and over periods from 1 to 5 s for sites with vs≤1,450 ft/s (softer soils).\n\nThe two-thirds factor is applied to the MCER-based parameters (SMS and SM1) to produce the design spectrum parameters (SDS and SD1) used in seismic design calculations. Additionally, the design response spectrum is determined by multiplying the risk-targeted maximum considered earthquake (MCER) response spectrum by (2/3).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"ed3e4a48-9ec5-4d96-b6b5-2d0b8f4035c8","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:59:34.534Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7-22 section 21.3 design response spectrum MCER two-thirds factor Sa SaM","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Wj2oiHgqeZDLEdLyFTcomR","content":[{"title":"Is Continued Use of the Two-Period Design Spectrum in the Equivalent Lateral Force Procedure of Seismic Design Permitted by ASCE 7-22? - ICC","url":"https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf"},{"title":"Multi-Period Response Spectrum","url":"https://www.oregon.gov/bcd/codes-stand/Documents/sam-24-03-multi-period-response.pdf"},{"title":"S. K. Ghosh Associates LLC - Seismic & Building Code Consulting","url":"https://www.skghoshassociates.com/blog/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1"},{"title":"Ground Motion Levels per ASCE 7-16 with ideCAD","url":"https://help.idecad.com/ideCAD/earthquake-ground-motion-levels-1"},{"title":"Multi-Period Response Spectra and Design Parameters (of ...","url":"https://files.scec.org/s3fs-public/2018_UGMS_1300_Kircher_BSSC_multi-period_spectra_and_design_parameters.pdf?.ArgamOa6ZWLtsDekb9Dn6w1Lg6VBYDC="},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Spectra Development","url":"https://quakemanager.eqsols.com/topics/idh-asce-7-22.htm"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788"},{"title":"Response spectrum curves (ASCE)","url":"https://www.graitec.com/Help/Advance_Design/En/response_spectrum_curves_(asce).htm"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about ASCE 7-22 Section 21.3 and the two-thirds factor relationship with MCER response spectra:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nWhere a site-specific ground motion analysis is performed in accordance with Section 11.4.7, the design response spectrum is to be as indicated in Section 21.3.\n\nRegarding the two-thirds factor and the relationship between SDS/Sa and MCER parameters:\n\nIn ASCE 7-22, SDS is (2/3)SMS and SMS is taken as 90% of the maximum spectral response acceleration, Sa, at any period within the range from 0.2 to 5 s, in accordance with ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4.\n\nSimilarly, SD1 is (2/3) SM1 and SM1 is defined as 90% of the maximum value of the product TSa over periods from 1 to 2 s for sites with shear wave velocity, vs>1,450 ft/s (stiff to moderately stiff soils), and over periods from 1 to 5 s for sites with vs≤1,450 ft/s (softer soils).\n\nThe two-thirds factor is applied to the MCER-based parameters (SMS and SM1) to produce the design spectrum parameters (SDS and SD1) used in seismic design calculations. Additionally, the design response spectrum is determined by multiplying the risk-targeted maximum considered earthquake (MCER) response spectrum by (2/3)."],"durationSeconds":6.405212843000001}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2gPrZZ1MU4imAgorUmvQk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7-22 section 11.4.7 site-specific ground motion scale factors 1.2 1.25 1.3 maximum horizontal response\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Ground Motions - A Rational Approach for Structural Engineers | ASCE\",\"url\":\"https://www.asce.org/communities/institutes-and-technical-groups/structural-engineering-institute/news/asce-7-22-ground-motions---a-rational-approach-for-structural-engineers\"},{\"title\":\"GROUND MOTION SELECTION GUIDE\",\"url\":\"https://slc.eeri.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Ground-Motion-Selection-Guide-2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11\"},{\"title\":\"Frequently Asked Seismic Questions\",\"url\":\"https://www.structuremag.org/article/frequently-asked-seismic-questions/\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 22 - Seismic Ground Motion and Long-Period Transition Maps | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s22\"},{\"title\":\"Design Ground Motions | U.S. Geological Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/design-ground-motions\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found some relevant information about site-specific ground motion scale factors in ASCE 7-22, though the specific section 11.4.7 with the exact values 1.2, 1.25, and 1.3 are referenced in ASCE 7-16 materials.\n\nThe intent of the exception defining site-specific MCER ground motions solely in terms of probabilistic MCER ground motions is to preclude unnecessary calculation when peak MCER response spectral accelerations are less than 1.2Fa.\n\nAdditionally, probabilistic ground motion response spectrum can be used when the largest spectral response acceleration of the probabilistic ground motion response spectrum is less than 1.2Fa.\n\nThe search results indicate that these scale factors are used in determining when site-specific ground motion analysis is required. The values you mentioned (1.2, 1.25, 1.3) appear to be threshold factors for evaluating maximum horizontal response accelerations in relation to the site amplification factor (Fa) to determine whether detailed site-specific analysis is necessary.\n\nFor more specific details about ASCE 7-22 section 11.4.7, you may need to consult the full ASCE 7-22 standard directly, as the publicly available excerpts focus more on the procedural requirements rather than the specific numerical thresholds you're asking about.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"a48615fe-3f99-469a-b6d4-28d9b1488a92","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:59:34.739Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7-22 section 11.4.7 site-specific ground motion scale factors 1.2 1.25 1.3 maximum horizontal response","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01BZYZd8BmQDbSbyhWn4r2Ff","content":[{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Ground Motions - A Rational Approach for Structural Engineers | ASCE","url":"https://www.asce.org/communities/institutes-and-technical-groups/structural-engineering-institute/news/asce-7-22-ground-motions---a-rational-approach-for-structural-engineers"},{"title":"GROUND MOTION SELECTION GUIDE","url":"https://slc.eeri.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Ground-Motion-Selection-Guide-2024.pdf"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11"},{"title":"Frequently Asked Seismic Questions","url":"https://www.structuremag.org/article/frequently-asked-seismic-questions/"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 22 - Seismic Ground Motion and Long-Period Transition Maps | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s22"},{"title":"Design Ground Motions | U.S. Geological Survey","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/design-ground-motions"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found some relevant information about site-specific ground motion scale factors in ASCE 7-22, though the specific section 11.4.7 with the exact values 1.2, 1.25, and 1.3 are referenced in ASCE 7-16 materials.\n\nThe intent of the exception defining site-specific MCER ground motions solely in terms of probabilistic MCER ground motions is to preclude unnecessary calculation when peak MCER response spectral accelerations are less than 1.2Fa.\n\nAdditionally, probabilistic ground motion response spectrum can be used when the largest spectral response acceleration of the probabilistic ground motion response spectrum is less than 1.2Fa.\n\nThe search results indicate that these scale factors are used in determining when site-specific ground motion analysis is required. The values you mentioned (1.2, 1.25, 1.3) appear to be threshold factors for evaluating maximum horizontal response accelerations in relation to the site amplification factor (Fa) to determine whether detailed site-specific analysis is necessary.\n\nFor more specific details about ASCE 7-22 section 11.4.7, you may need to consult the full ASCE 7-22 standard directly, as the publicly available excerpts focus more on the procedural requirements rather than the specific numerical thresholds you're asking about."],"durationSeconds":5.484314810000003}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CiXJzJFYG8gzKybMHG2Bmt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7-22 section 21.4 SDS SD1 site-specific design acceleration parameters formulas\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4\",\"url\":\"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Is Continued Use of the Two-Period Design Spectrum in the Equivalent Lateral Force Procedure of Seismic Design Permitted by ASCE 7-22? - ICC\",\"url\":\"https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html\"},{\"title\":\"Design Example 3 Site-specific Ground Motion Procedures ...\",\"url\":\"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S181-Sample.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou\"},{\"title\":\"New Site-Specific Ground Motion Requirements of ASCE 7-16\",\"url\":\"https://cdn.ymaws.com/seaoc.site-ym.com/resource/resmgr/Convention_Proceedings/2017/56R1_PAPER_Kircher_R1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11\"},{\"title\":\"Page 1 of 18 CHANGES IN SEISMIC DESIGN CRITERIA ...\",\"url\":\"https://seau.starchapter.com/images/downloads/SEAUNews_Documents/asce7_16workshop_whitepaper_20200131_final.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I can provide you with information about ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4 regarding SDS and SD1 site-specific design acceleration parameters:\n\n## Key Information on SDS and SD1 in ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4\n\nIn ASCE 7-22, SDS is (2/3) SMS and SMS is taken as 90% of the maximum spectral response acceleration, Sa, at any period within the range from 0.2 to 5 s, in accordance with ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4.\n\n### Related Parameters\n\nTwo additional parameters SDS and SD1 are used to define design spectral accelerations. Values of the seismic parameters SDS and SD1 (2/3SMS and 2/3SM1) are provided online by the USGS Seismic Design Web Service, for user-specified site location (e.g., latitude and longitude) and site class.\n\n### Application in Design\n\nAccording to Section 21.4, for use with the equivalent lateral force procedure, the site-specific acceleration, Sa, at T shall be permitted to replace SD1/T in Equation 12.8-3 and SD1TL/T2 in Equation 12.8-4.\n\n### Design Response Spectrum Application\n\nIn accordance with Section 21.3, the design site-specific spectral response acceleration at any period shall not be less than 80 percent of the Sa determined in accordance with Section 11.4.6.\n\nFor a comprehensive review of the specific formulas in Section 21.4, you would need to consult the actual ASCE 7-22 standard document directly, as the search results focus on the application and definition of these parameters rather than the complete set of formulas.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"82aebb69-e022-4deb-9e65-c483f372d9d1","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:59:35.621Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7-22 section 21.4 SDS SD1 site-specific design acceleration parameters formulas","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01NJpf2uoi6uraqVEKWUKsPz","content":[{"title":"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4","url":"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf"},{"title":"Is Continued Use of the Two-Period Design Spectrum in the Equivalent Lateral Force Procedure of Seismic Design Permitted by ASCE 7-22? - ICC","url":"https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/"},{"title":"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation","url":"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html"},{"title":"Design Example 3 Site-specific Ground Motion Procedures ...","url":"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S181-Sample.pdf"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou"},{"title":"New Site-Specific Ground Motion Requirements of ASCE 7-16","url":"https://cdn.ymaws.com/seaoc.site-ym.com/resource/resmgr/Convention_Proceedings/2017/56R1_PAPER_Kircher_R1.pdf"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11"},{"title":"Page 1 of 18 CHANGES IN SEISMIC DESIGN CRITERIA ...","url":"https://seau.starchapter.com/images/downloads/SEAUNews_Documents/asce7_16workshop_whitepaper_20200131_final.pdf"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21"}]},"Based on the search results, I can provide you with information about ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4 regarding SDS and SD1 site-specific design acceleration parameters:\n\n## Key Information on SDS and SD1 in ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4\n\nIn ASCE 7-22, SDS is (2/3) SMS and SMS is taken as 90% of the maximum spectral response acceleration, Sa, at any period within the range from 0.2 to 5 s, in accordance with ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4.\n\n### Related Parameters\n\nTwo additional parameters SDS and SD1 are used to define design spectral accelerations. Values of the seismic parameters SDS and SD1 (2/3SMS and 2/3SM1) are provided online by the USGS Seismic Design Web Service, for user-specified site location (e.g., latitude and longitude) and site class.\n\n### Application in Design\n\nAccording to Section 21.4, for use with the equivalent lateral force procedure, the site-specific acceleration, Sa, at T shall be permitted to replace SD1/T in Equation 12.8-3 and SD1TL/T2 in Equation 12.8-4.\n\n### Design Response Spectrum Application\n\nIn accordance with Section 21.3, the design site-specific spectral response acceleration at any period shall not be less than 80 percent of the Sa determined in accordance with Section 11.4.6.\n\nFor a comprehensive review of the specific formulas in Section 21.4, you would need to consult the actual ASCE 7-22 standard document directly, as the search results focus on the application and definition of these parameters rather than the complete set of formulas."],"durationSeconds":7.2478775739999985}}
