{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017mHaboCoT3XSRVSWa5DpSv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Bazzurro Cornell 1999 disaggregation seismic hazard magnitude distance epsilon\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\",\"url\":\"http://sismologia.ist.utl.pt/~sismologia.daemon/files/Bazurro_and_Cornell_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America | GeoScienceWorld\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Statewide California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://central.scec.org/publication/407\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Southern California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://www.scec.org/publication/407\"},{\"title\":\"Researchgate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Paolo_Bazzurro/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard/links/54202dc10cf203f155c2afe3/Disaggregation-of-Seismic-Hazard.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard (1999) | Paolo Bazzurro | 518 Citations\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/papers/disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-3e6zx96xtw\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-Bazzurro-Cornell/907cde3d1c6f6ca8b6e79ded693847be6abe6866\"},{\"title\":\"1 SHORT COMMUNICATION\",\"url\":\"https://files01.core.ac.uk/download/pdf/77012286.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard – ScienceOpen\",\"url\":\"https://www.scienceopen.com/document?vid=07a4da96-8960-4537-bcb0-a818a3898b03\"}]\n\nI've successfully found the paper you were searching for. Here's what I found:\n\n## Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) - Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\n\nThe paper \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\" was published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America in April 1999, volume 89, issue 2, pages 501–520.\n\n### Key Information:\n\nProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) integrates over all potential earthquake occurrences and ground motions to estimate the mean frequency of exceedance of any given spectral acceleration at the site. For improved communication and insights, it is becoming common practice to display the relative contributions to that hazard from the range of values of magnitude, M, distance, R, and epsilon, ɛ, the number of standard deviations from the median ground motion as predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\nThe proposed disaggregation procedures, while conceptually similar, differ in several important points that are often not reported by the researchers and not appreciated by the users.\n\nThe paper discusses issues such as definition of the probability distribution to be disaggregated, different disaggregation techniques, disaggregation of R versus ln R, and the effects of different binning strategies on the results. The authors propose to improve the disaggregation process by displaying hazard contributions in terms of not R, but latitude, longitude, as well as M and ɛ.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"03474d08-ca55-4a3e-87a3-2d099d47f1a3","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:59:44.012Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Bazzurro Cornell 1999 disaggregation seismic hazard magnitude distance epsilon","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Sfsk2CUey9by8FL35et8t1","content":[{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard","url":"http://sismologia.ist.utl.pt/~sismologia.daemon/files/Bazurro_and_Cornell_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America | GeoScienceWorld","url":"https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard"},{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Statewide California Earthquake Center","url":"https://central.scec.org/publication/407"},{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Southern California Earthquake Center","url":"https://www.scec.org/publication/407"},{"title":"Researchgate","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Paolo_Bazzurro/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard/links/54202dc10cf203f155c2afe3/Disaggregation-of-Seismic-Hazard.pdf"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard (1999) | Paolo Bazzurro | 518 Citations","url":"https://typeset.io/papers/disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-3e6zx96xtw"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-Bazzurro-Cornell/907cde3d1c6f6ca8b6e79ded693847be6abe6866"},{"title":"1 SHORT COMMUNICATION","url":"https://files01.core.ac.uk/download/pdf/77012286.pdf"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard – ScienceOpen","url":"https://www.scienceopen.com/document?vid=07a4da96-8960-4537-bcb0-a818a3898b03"}]},"I've successfully found the paper you were searching for. Here's what I found:\n\n## Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) - Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\n\nThe paper \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\" was published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America in April 1999, volume 89, issue 2, pages 501–520.\n\n### Key Information:\n\nProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) integrates over all potential earthquake occurrences and ground motions to estimate the mean frequency of exceedance of any given spectral acceleration at the site. For improved communication and insights, it is becoming common practice to display the relative contributions to that hazard from the range of values of magnitude, M, distance, R, and epsilon, ɛ, the number of standard deviations from the median ground motion as predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\nThe proposed disaggregation procedures, while conceptually similar, differ in several important points that are often not reported by the researchers and not appreciated by the users.\n\nThe paper discusses issues such as definition of the probability distribution to be disaggregated, different disaggregation techniques, disaggregation of R versus ln R, and the effects of different binning strategies on the results. The authors propose to improve the disaggregation process by displaying hazard contributions in terms of not R, but latitude, longitude, as well as M and ɛ."],"durationSeconds":4.1204322249999965}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012XnecePMFagHEPjPKzM6kj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Cornell 1968 probabilistic seismic hazard analysis PSHA hazard integral formulation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) A Primer\",\"url\":\"https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Why is Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) still used? - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0031920116303016\"},{\"title\":\"A Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis Method Incorporating Physics-Based Simulation and Ground Motion Prediction Equation | International Journal of Disaster Risk Science\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13753-025-00640-7\"},{\"title\":\"Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Assessment (PSHA)\",\"url\":\"https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html\"},{\"title\":\"Philosophical aspects of probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA): a critical review | Natural Hazards | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11069-023-05901-6\"},{\"title\":\"Probabilistic seismic hazard analysis of the sites of critical objects\",\"url\":\"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2534/70_short_paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"INTRODUCTION TO PROBABILISTIC SEISMIC HAZARD ...\",\"url\":\"https://scits.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj22081/files/media/file/baker_2013_intro_psha_v2_0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) “Introduction to Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis” (Extended version of contribution by A. Kijko, Encyclopedia of Solid Earth Geophysics, Harsh Gupta (Ed.), Springer, 2011).\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/274835429_Introduction_to_Probabilistic_Seismic_Hazard_Analysis_Extended_version_of_contribution_by_A_Kijko_Encyclopedia_of_Solid_Earth_Geophysics_Harsh_Gupta_Ed_Springer_2011\"},{\"title\":\"Physically based probabilistic seismic hazard analysis using broadband ground motion simulation: a case study for the Prince Islands Fault, Marmara Sea | Earth, Planets and Space | Full Text\",\"url\":\"https://earth-planets-space.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s40623-016-0520-3\"},{\"title\":\"An efficient, analytic solution using order statistics for probabilistic seismic‐hazard assessment without the Poisson assumption | U.S. Geological Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/efficient-analytic-solution-using-order-statistics-probabilistic-seismic-hazard\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here is information about Cornell 1968 and probabilistic seismic hazard analysis:\n\n## Cornell 1968 PSHA Foundation\n\nThe numerical/analytical approach to PSHA was first formalized by Cornell (1968). More specifically, as a computational procedure the PSHA approach was first introduced by Cornell (1968) for peak ground acceleration (PGA).\n\n## PSHA Definition and Goals\n\nThe goal of probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) is to quantify the rate (or probability) of exceeding various ground-motion levels at a site (or a map of sites) given all possible earthquakes.\n\n## The Hazard Integral Formulation\n\nThe classical PSHA methodology was introduced by C. Allin Cornell in his milestone paper of 1968. The core of PSHA lies in the integration of individual influences of potential earthquake sources (considering both size and distance) into the probability distribution of the maximum annual ground motion parameter, from which the return period follows.\n\n## Key Assumptions\n\nStandard approaches to probabilistic seismic hazard assessment (PSHA) assume that earthquakes are random, independent events that follow a Poisson distribution of occurrences in a given time period (Cornell, 1968).\n\n## The Classic Procedure\n\nThe classic procedure known as Cornell-McGuire procedure for the PSHA includes four steps, which form the foundation of modern PSHA methodology.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"991f719d-b05f-4ee0-9f89-cb0872448ebb","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:59:45.230Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Cornell 1968 probabilistic seismic hazard analysis PSHA hazard integral formulation","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01QVMroabMpJWbaYuqv5ggzZ","content":[{"title":"Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) A Primer","url":"https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf"},{"title":"Why is Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) still used? - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0031920116303016"},{"title":"A Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis Method Incorporating Physics-Based Simulation and Ground Motion Prediction Equation | International Journal of Disaster Risk Science","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13753-025-00640-7"},{"title":"Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Assessment (PSHA)","url":"https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html"},{"title":"Philosophical aspects of probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA): a critical review | Natural Hazards | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11069-023-05901-6"},{"title":"Probabilistic seismic hazard analysis of the sites of critical objects","url":"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2534/70_short_paper.pdf"},{"title":"INTRODUCTION TO PROBABILISTIC SEISMIC HAZARD ...","url":"https://scits.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj22081/files/media/file/baker_2013_intro_psha_v2_0.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) “Introduction to Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis” (Extended version of contribution by A. Kijko, Encyclopedia of Solid Earth Geophysics, Harsh Gupta (Ed.), Springer, 2011).","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/274835429_Introduction_to_Probabilistic_Seismic_Hazard_Analysis_Extended_version_of_contribution_by_A_Kijko_Encyclopedia_of_Solid_Earth_Geophysics_Harsh_Gupta_Ed_Springer_2011"},{"title":"Physically based probabilistic seismic hazard analysis using broadband ground motion simulation: a case study for the Prince Islands Fault, Marmara Sea | Earth, Planets and Space | Full Text","url":"https://earth-planets-space.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s40623-016-0520-3"},{"title":"An efficient, analytic solution using order statistics for probabilistic seismic‐hazard assessment without the Poisson assumption | U.S. Geological Survey","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/efficient-analytic-solution-using-order-statistics-probabilistic-seismic-hazard"}]},"Based on the web search results, here is information about Cornell 1968 and probabilistic seismic hazard analysis:\n\n## Cornell 1968 PSHA Foundation\n\nThe numerical/analytical approach to PSHA was first formalized by Cornell (1968). More specifically, as a computational procedure the PSHA approach was first introduced by Cornell (1968) for peak ground acceleration (PGA).\n\n## PSHA Definition and Goals\n\nThe goal of probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) is to quantify the rate (or probability) of exceeding various ground-motion levels at a site (or a map of sites) given all possible earthquakes.\n\n## The Hazard Integral Formulation\n\nThe classical PSHA methodology was introduced by C. Allin Cornell in his milestone paper of 1968. The core of PSHA lies in the integration of individual influences of potential earthquake sources (considering both size and distance) into the probability distribution of the maximum annual ground motion parameter, from which the return period follows.\n\n## Key Assumptions\n\nStandard approaches to probabilistic seismic hazard assessment (PSHA) assume that earthquakes are random, independent events that follow a Poisson distribution of occurrences in a given time period (Cornell, 1968).\n\n## The Classic Procedure\n\nThe classic procedure known as Cornell-McGuire procedure for the PSHA includes four steps, which form the foundation of modern PSHA methodology."],"durationSeconds":6.0903167259999975}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lxmron8VizLnTnRzkt9NoV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"GISTM Global Industry Standard Tailings Management 2020 seismic design earthquake AEP consequence classification\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GLOBAL INDUSTRY STANDARD ON TAILINGS MANAGEMENT AUGUST 2020\",\"url\":\"https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM): A Complete Overview\",\"url\":\"https://blog.gistm.net/blog/a1-understanding-gistm-complete-overview/\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management: Major Improvement or “Death by a Thousand Codes”? - ATC Williams\",\"url\":\"https://atcwilliams.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-major-improvement-or-death-by-a-thousand-codes/\"},{\"title\":\"ICMM - Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management\",\"url\":\"https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)\",\"url\":\"https://www.goldfields.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-gistm.php\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)\",\"url\":\"https://www.hydro.com/globalassets/04-sustainability/2023-07-12-gistm-disclosure-15-1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Practical steps to Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM) compliance for operational tailings storage facilities in South Africa\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)\",\"url\":\"https://geosyntec.com/markets/mining/gistm\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management Public ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2023/230804_bhpgistmconformancestatementandpublicdisclosure.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management - Global Tailings Review\",\"url\":\"https://globaltailingsreview.org/global-industry-standard/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's information about GISTM Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management 2020 regarding seismic design, earthquake, AEP (Annual Exceedance Probability), and consequence classification:\n\n## Overview\nThe GISTM was launched in August 2020 by the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), the Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI), and the International Council on Mining and Metals (ICMM), and comprises six topic areas, 15 principles and 77 auditable requirements.\n\n## Consequence Classification\nThe consequence of failure classification of the tailings facility is determined by assessing the downstream conditions and selecting the classification corresponding to the highest Consequence Classification for each category.\n\n## Seismic Design and Earthquake Requirements\nThe GISTM establishes significant seismic design requirements based on consequence classification. Implementation of an \"Extreme\" failure consequence category requires design for potentially significantly higher design loads—earthquakes with a 10,000 year return period. \n\nIn regions with low seismic activity like South Africa, most tailings dams are located within regions with a peak ground acceleration (PGA) of between 0.2 and 0.4 m²/s with a 10% chance of exceedance in 50 years (1 in 475 years return period), and the new GISTM values represent significantly higher requirements than previously adopted standards.\n\n## Implementation Timeline\nThe ICMM has allowed for a three-year implementation period (August 2023) for Extreme and Very high-consequence facilities and two additional years for other facilities (August 2025).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"c721db24-7133-404e-b3d5-4db49fdf56a0","timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:59:46.056Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"GISTM Global Industry Standard Tailings Management 2020 seismic design earthquake AEP consequence classification","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01BuZxiFJyWoYH4hcX6CmDqS","content":[{"title":"GLOBAL INDUSTRY STANDARD ON TAILINGS MANAGEMENT AUGUST 2020","url":"https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf"},{"title":"Understanding the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM): A Complete Overview","url":"https://blog.gistm.net/blog/a1-understanding-gistm-complete-overview/"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management: Major Improvement or “Death by a Thousand Codes”? - ATC Williams","url":"https://atcwilliams.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-major-improvement-or-death-by-a-thousand-codes/"},{"title":"ICMM - Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://www.goldfields.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-gistm.php"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://www.hydro.com/globalassets/04-sustainability/2023-07-12-gistm-disclosure-15-1.pdf"},{"title":"Practical steps to Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM) compliance for operational tailings storage facilities in South Africa","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://geosyntec.com/markets/mining/gistm"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management Public ...","url":"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2023/230804_bhpgistmconformancestatementandpublicdisclosure.pdf"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management - Global Tailings Review","url":"https://globaltailingsreview.org/global-industry-standard/"}]},"Based on the web search results, here's information about GISTM Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management 2020 regarding seismic design, earthquake, AEP (Annual Exceedance Probability), and consequence classification:\n\n## Overview\nThe GISTM was launched in August 2020 by the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), the Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI), and the International Council on Mining and Metals (ICMM), and comprises six topic areas, 15 principles and 77 auditable requirements.\n\n## Consequence Classification\nThe consequence of failure classification of the tailings facility is determined by assessing the downstream conditions and selecting the classification corresponding to the highest Consequence Classification for each category.\n\n## Seismic Design and Earthquake Requirements\nThe GISTM establishes significant seismic design requirements based on consequence classification. Implementation of an \"Extreme\" failure consequence category requires design for potentially significantly higher design loads—earthquakes with a 10,000 year return period. \n\nIn regions with low seismic activity like South Africa, most tailings dams are located within regions with a peak ground acceleration (PGA) of between 0.2 and 0.4 m²/s with a 10% chance of exceedance in 50 years (1 in 475 years return period), and the new GISTM values represent significantly higher requirements than previously adopted standards.\n\n## Implementation Timeline\nThe ICMM has allowed for a three-year implementation period (August 2023) for Extreme and Very high-consequence facilities and two additional years for other facilities (August 2025)."],"durationSeconds":7.204226079}}
