eed to preserve those while adding my audit footnote separately after the paragraph ends. Looking at line 21, the paragraph contains an inline footnote marked with `^[...]` that's part of the original content, so I'll keep that intact and place the audit footnote after the full paragraph concludes. I'm working through how to position the audit footnote in Pandoc syntax—it needs to come after the original inline footnote that's attached to the equation reference, right at the end of the paragraph before the display math begins. Looking at the site.qmd file structure now, I can see it starts with YAML front matter, then has three paragraphs about near-surface geology and site effects, followed by a display equation for the amplification model around line 13. I'm working through the mathematical formulation for site amplification effects, defining how the spectral acceleration gets modified based on local soil conditions and establishing an envelope function that captures the maximum amplification across different return periods. The second approach uses a reference-rock hazard curve at a specific shear-wave velocity, then applies an amplification model that captures how softer sites amplify ground motion at longer periods while experiencing deamplification at shorter periods, with the amplification factor itself treated as a lognormal random variable defined by its mean and standard deviation. The three dispersion components—linear site-term, inter-reference, and nonlinear—are all period-dependent and available in the published model, with their functional forms detailed in the site model section. The site amplification factor then scales the reference spectral acceleration by the period and soil velocity, and I'm now moving through the calculation for each return period and oscillator period. $S_a^{F}$ is the spectral ordinate from the amplification model applied to the reference-rock hazard curve. This envelope approach ensures the more conservative estimate controls the design spectrum at each period, since GMPEs can underpredict where their empirical site terms lack sufficient data, and the amplification model may miss near-field or source-specific effects that the reference-rock curve doesn't capture. By taking the maximum at every period, we avoid the risk of unconservative design. Now I'm moving into the site classification section, which introduces the Vs30 parameter and its formula, then explains how it's used in NEHRP site classifications with a breakdown of classes A through E.\n\nThe next part covers three major building code standards—Australian AS 1726, ASCE/SEI 7-22, and NBC-2020—each with a brief explanation followed by a table that outlines their respective site classification schemes.\n\nFor the bullet list of site classes A through F earlier in the document, I'm treating it as a single cohesive block rather than auditing each item individually, since they form one connected list under the same section.\n\nLooking at the raw file structure, I can see the paragraph about NEHRP provisions is actually separate from the bullet list—they're divided by a blank line in Markdown. So I need to audit them as distinct blocks: the introductory paragraph gets one footnote, the entire bullet list gets another, and then the following paragraph about Classes A-E gets its own footnote.\n\nNow I'm seeing the continuation of that list with the specific velocity ranges for each site class, from the hard rock of Class A down through the soft soil of Class E, each with their corresponding Vs30 thresholds. I'm working through how to handle footnotes for this list structure. Since the bullet points aren't separated by blank lines, they form a single contiguous block in Markdown, so I'll place one audit footnote after the entire list rather than after each item. Now I need to write out the chunks efficiently, starting with the URLs.\n\nGathering the reference links for the ASCE standards and related resources... I'm compiling a collection of reference materials on seismic hazard analysis and tailings management standards, pulling together technical resources from various authoritative sources including USGS, Stanford, Berkeley, and international standards organizations. I'm confirming the standard references in this field and adding DOIs where I can find them—Bazzurro & Cornell's 1999 work and Cornell's original 1968 paper from the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, though I need to verify the exact DOI for that earlier publication.\n\nNow I'm moving forward to draft the six chunks with specific attention to technical corrections: fixing the circular notation in the first chunk where the equation should reference different variables, catching a language error in the equation context, addressing grammatical issues in the chapter reference, and standardizing the subscript notation throughout chunk three to maintain consistency.\n\nI also need to verify the BSSC citation—the 2015 reference points to older NEHRP provisions, while the 2020 edition (FEMA P-2082) represents the most current guidance, which would be more appropriate for this professional context. I'm verifying the Vs30 thresholds are correct—those values match the standard NEHRP classification, and while the 2020 provisions expanded it to eight classes, the traditional A-F system is still widely used. Now I need to write out all six chunk files and assemble them, but first I should carefully check the exact line structure of each file to make sure footnotes are placed correctly. The design response spectrum must follow Section 21.3 procedures, with acceleration parameters determined per Section 21.4. The probabilistic spectral accelerations represent the 5% damped response spectrum with a 1% collapse probability over 50 years, and if the ground motion models don't capture maximum horizontal response, I need to apply scaling factors—1.2 for periods ≤0.2s, 1.25 for longer periods—to account for the geometric mean of the two horizontal components.\n\nFor the design-level spectral accelerations, I'm applying a 2/3 scaling factor to the MCER ordinates, and when using site-specific procedures, I'm calculating the design acceleration parameters using the maximum response approach. Now I'm defining the spectral response acceleration parameters at short periods and at 1 second, which are derived by scaling the design parameters by a factor of 1.5. I'm working through the footnote placement for paragraph 6 — since there's no blank line between the closing math delimiter and the start of paragraph 7, I need to insert the footnote right after the `$$` ends, before the new paragraph begins. I'm overthinking the footnote placement—what matters is clarity and consistency. I'll put each footnote right after the paragraph it audits, with a line break if needed to keep things readable. Now let me actually write out these chunk files instead of continuing to deliberate. The procedures outlined in §11.4.7 require applying the methodologies in §21.1 and §21.2 when conducting site response analysis and ground motion hazard analysis to establish design ground motions.\n\nWhen either §21.1 or §21.2 is applied, the design response spectrum follows §21.3 specifications while design acceleration parameters are determined per §21.4.\n\nNow I'm looking at how probabilistic spectral response accelerations are defined—specifically the 5% damped acceleration response spectrum representing maximum horizontal response with a 1% probability of collapse threshold.\n\nIf the ground motion models don't directly capture maximum horizontal response, I need to apply scaling factors to the computed spectral accelerations. The scaling depends on the period: 1.2 for periods up to 0.2 seconds, 1.25 at 1.0 second, and 1.3 for periods at or above 10 seconds, assuming the models predict geometric mean or similar metrics of the two horizontal components.\n\nThe risk-targeted framework with a 1% collapse probability over 50 years is foundational to ASCE 7-22, and the 5% critical damping assumption is standard across building codes, though the specific scale factor values at those period breakpoints come from the licensed ASCE 7-22 text itself and couldn't be independently verified from public sources. To get the design-level spectral accelerations, I scale the MCER values by 2/3 per the code equation. When using a site-specific procedure, I calculate the design acceleration parameters $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$ by taking the maximum spectral acceleration across specified period ranges and applying a 0.9 factor, with $S_{D1}$ also constrained by the acceleration at 1 second and adjusted based on average shear wave velocity. I notice there's a language error where \"con\" appears in the conditional definition—that's Spanish and needs to be replaced with English. Now I'm working through the spectral response acceleration parameters, where $S_{MS}$ and $S_{M1}$ are derived from the design spectrum values using established relationships that check out against USGS and ICC Safe sources.\n\nHowever, there's a critical typo in the second equation: it reads $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$, which is just a circular identity and doesn't convey anything useful. The correct form should be $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$, following from the standard relationship $S_{D1} = \\frac{2}{3}S_{M1}$. This is a significant error that could mislead anyone using these equations downstream.\n\nNow I'm looking at how three major international standards—the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management, the Canadian Dam Association guidelines, and the Australian National Committee on Large Dams—approach seismic design for tailings storage facilities. Each one uses consequence classification and lifecycle phase to set performance objectives and design earthquake criteria, with consequence classes ranging from Low to Extreme based on factors like population at risk, potential loss of life, and environmental impacts. The specific classification criteria and corresponding design earthquake probabilities are detailed in the appendix tables.\n\nFor each consequence class, the design earthquake requirements shift depending on the facility's lifecycle phase—construction, operation, closure, and post-closure—since each phase has different engineering conditions and risk profiles. The design ground motions are organized around three principal earthquake levels: operational, closure, and post-closure stages. During operations, the annual exceedance probabilities vary significantly across the three standards and consequence classes, ranging from 1/100 to 1/10,000, but this variation narrows considerably in the post-closure phase, where all three frameworks converge on the most stringent criterion—the Maximum Credible Earthquake or the 1/10,000 annual exceedance probability.\n\nThe three standards take structurally different approaches to implementing these requirements. GISTM specifies a single earthquake design ground motion for each consequence class and phase, with the MCE serving as an upper bound if it produces higher demands; notably, it applies the same annual exceedance probabilities during operations and active closure before stepping up to a uniform 1/10,000 for all consequence classes at passive closure. CDA uses the Safety Evaluation Earthquake as the design basis with the MCE as an upper bound for high-consequence facilities, progressively tightening the annual exceedance probabilities from operations through closure. I'm organizing the lifecycle phases of tailings storage facilities—moving through construction, active operations, and then closure with post-closure requirements, where design criteria typically become more stringent than during operational phases. I'm also verifying the GISTM 2020 reference to ensure it's properly cited. One key thing I want to flag in chunk 3 about the disaggregation equation—there are some notation inconsistencies I'm catching. The subscript uses lowercase `l` in one place but `\\ell` elsewhere, and there's a missing comma in the lambda term that should separate the indices. I need to clean these up for consistency.\n\nAlso, that summation at the end looks garbled—the `[I>i^{*}]=1` notation doesn't quite make sense as written. It seems like it's trying to show that the probabilities sum to 1, but the expression got mangled somewhere.\n\nLooking at the marginal distribution equations next, I see the same inconsistency with `\\theta_{j,k,l}` appearing again with the lowercase `l` instead of `\\ell`, and the second equation appears to be cut off mid-line. I'm ready to rewrite the next thinking. Since the current rewritten thinking discusses notation inconsistencies in mathematical formulas (the use of lowercase `l` versus `\\ell` in subscripts, and the correctness of the marginal formulas), I'm prepared to continue naturally from there.\n\nHowever, I notice the next thinking you've provided is just \". For real this time.\" — which appears to be a fragment or meta-comment rather than substantive thinking content. Based on the guidelines, if the next thinking is empty or incoherent, I should output nothing or describe what's happening naturally.\n\nSince this fragment doesn't contain actual thinking to rewrite, I'll output nothing and await the actual next thinking chunk.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5716,"cache_read_input_tokens":54995,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5716,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f349b4-04df-4e80-9dd6-0658f00ba709","uuid":"aee1ada4-6c67-40ee-a285-dc53ef3f57b2"}
